01:13:03 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: The short answer is: **none**. There is no credible scientific evidence that cracking your knuckles, even daily for decades, causes arthritis. One of the most famous studies on this is a self-experiment by Dr. Donald Unger. He cracked the knuckles on only his left hand at least twice a day for over 60 years, leaving his right hand alone. After 60 years, he had no arthritis in either hand and no visible difference between them. (He won an Ig Nobel Prize for this work.) Larger observational studies have also found no association between habitual knuckle cracking and the development of osteoarthritis in the hands. The sound you hear isn't bones grinding or popping out of place; it's simply the rapid release of gas bubbles (mostly nitrogen) from the synovial fluid that lubricates your joints. This process is considered mechanically harmless. So you can crack your knuckles every day without worrying about setting yourself up for arthritis. The only mild, long-term risks that have been occasionally reported in heavy crackers are slightly reduced grip strength or temporary swelling, but these are minor and unrelated to arthritis.
